IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ucta-nouns`
IMPORT `ucta-logic`
IMPORT `ucta-schedules`

-- Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation.
-- LAYER 1, MODULE 5: PART 1, EXPLANATORY PROVISIONS, sections 11 to 14
-- ("Reasonableness" test; Dealing as consumer; Varieties of exemption clause; Interpretation of this Part).
-- It comes before sections 1 to 10 in the import order because those sections use these definitions.
--
-- READING A RESULT (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").
--   * JUST FALSE : the text says no in its own words (a definition that "means", a flat negative,
--                  a positive effect whose "if" or "where" fails);
--   * JUST TRUE  : the text says yes in its own words;
--   * NOTHING    : not covered: silent, list not closed ("includes"), undefined term, a judgment
--                  or another Act the text points to, or the case falls outside what its own wording limits.
-- A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.

§ `Section 11 - the reasonableness test`

-- src:314 | 11.—(1) In relation to a contract term, the requirement of
-- src:315 | reasonableness for the purposes of this Part and section 3 of the
-- src:316 | Misrepresentation Act 1967 is that the term shall have been a fair and
-- src:317 | reasonable one to be included having regard to the circumstances
-- src:318 | which were, or ought reasonably to have been, known to or in the
-- src:319 | contemplation of the parties when the contract was made.
@ref s 11(1) (src:314-319)
GIVEN relied IS A `What is relied on`
      `it was fair and reasonable to include the term, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(1) the contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` MEANS
    CONSIDER relied
    -- TRUE-kind / FALSE-kind: "the requirement of reasonableness ... is that the term shall have been a fair and reasonable one to be included having regard to the circumstances which were, or ought reasonably to have been, known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made" -- the judgment is the court's, taken as a plain input (never guessed)
    WHEN `A contract term` THEN `it was fair and reasonable to include the term, having regard to the circumstances known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In relation to a contract term"; subsection (1) does not name a notice with contractual effect, and subsection (3) excludes it (see NOTES.md, fork Q-notice-with-contractual-effect)
    WHEN `A notice that has contractual effect` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In relation to a contract term"; a notice without contractual effect is dealt with in subsection (3)
    WHEN `A notice that does not have contractual effect` THEN NOTHING

-- src:332 | (3) In relation to a notice (not being a notice having contractual
-- src:333 | effect), the requirement of reasonableness under this Act is that it
-- src:334 | should be fair and reasonable to allow reliance on it, having regard to
-- src:335 | all the circumstances obtaining when the liability arose or (but for the
-- src:336 | notice) would have arisen.
@ref s 11(3) (src:332-336)
GIVEN relied IS A `What is relied on`
      `it is fair and reasonable to allow reliance on the notice, having regard to all the circumstances obtaining when the liability arose or (but for the notice) would have arisen` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(3) the notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` MEANS
    CONSIDER relied
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "In relation to a notice (not being a notice having contractual effect)"; this is a contract term
    WHEN `A contract term` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "(not being a notice having contractual effect)"
    WHEN `A notice that has contractual effect` THEN NOTHING
    -- TRUE-kind / FALSE-kind: "the requirement of reasonableness under this Act is that it should be fair and reasonable to allow reliance on it, having regard to all the circumstances obtaining when the liability arose or (but for the notice) would have arisen" -- the judgment is the court's, taken as a plain input
    WHEN `A notice that does not have contractual effect` THEN `it is fair and reasonable to allow reliance on the notice, having regard to all the circumstances obtaining when the liability arose or (but for the notice) would have arisen`

-- ss 11(1), 11(3) and 29(2) together: a contract term is taken as satisfying the requirement if
-- s 29(2) says so (a competent authority's approval), else by the court's judgment under s 11(1),
-- or s 11(3) for a notice without contractual effect.  Gathered by hand (option B, ruled 2026-11-04).
GIVEN relied IS A `What is relied on`
      judged IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the s 29(2) deeming` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`ss 11(1), 11(3) and 29(2) the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` MEANS
    CONSIDER relied
    WHEN `A contract term` THEN `the s 29(2) deeming` `or else` judged
    WHEN `A notice that has contractual effect` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `A notice that does not have contractual effect` THEN judged

-- src:321 | (2) In determining for the purposes of section 6 or 7 whether a
-- src:322 | contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness, regard shall
-- src:323 | be had in particular to the matters specified in the Second Schedule;
-- src:324 | but this subsection does not prevent the court or arbitrator from
-- src:325 | holding, in accordance with any rule of law, that a term which
-- src:326 | purports to exclude or restrict any relevant liability is not a term of the
-- src:327 | contract.
@ref s 11(2), first limb (src:321-327)
GIVEN `the determination is for the purposes of section 6 or 7` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `Guideline`)
`s 11(2) the matters to which regard is had in determining reasonableness under section 6 or 7` MEANS
    IF `the determination is for the purposes of section 6 or 7`
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "In determining for the purposes of section 6 or 7 whether a contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness, regard shall be had in particular to the matters specified in the Second Schedule"
    THEN JUST `sch 2 the guidelines`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "for the purposes of section 6 or 7" -- a scope limit with no "only"; for sections 2, 3 and 4 the subsection is silent
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:321 | (2) In determining for the purposes of section 6 or 7 whether a
-- src:322 | contract term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness, regard shall
-- src:323 | be had in particular to the matters specified in the Second Schedule;
-- src:324 | but this subsection does not prevent the court or arbitrator from
-- src:325 | holding, in accordance with any rule of law, that a term which
-- src:326 | purports to exclude or restrict any relevant liability is not a term of the
-- src:327 | contract.
@ref s 11(2), second limb (src:321-327)
GIVEN `the term purports to exclude or restrict a relevant liability` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the holding is in accordance with a rule of law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(2) the subsection prevents the court or arbitrator from holding that the term is not a term of the contract` MEANS
    IF `the term purports to exclude or restrict a relevant liability` AND `the holding is in accordance with a rule of law`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "this subsection does not prevent the court or arbitrator from holding, in accordance with any rule of law, that a term which purports to exclude or restrict any relevant liability is not a term of the contract"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks of "a term which purports to exclude or restrict any relevant liability" and a holding "in accordance with any rule of law"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:338 | (4) Where by reference to a contract term or notice a person seeks to
-- src:339 | restrict liability to a specified sum of money, and the question arises
-- src:340 | (under this or any other Act) whether the term or notice satisfies the
-- src:341 | requirement of reasonableness, regard shall be had in particular (but
-- src:342 | without prejudice to subsection (2) in the case of contract terms) to —
-- src:344 | (a) the resources which he could expect to be available to him
-- src:345 | for the purpose of meeting the liability should it arise; and
-- src:347 | (b) how far it was open to him to cover himself by insurance.
@ref s 11(4) (src:338-347)
GIVEN `a person seeks, by reference to a contract term or notice, to restrict liability to a specified sum of money` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the question arises whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(4) regard is to be had in particular to the resources and insurance matters` MEANS
    IF `a person seeks, by reference to a contract term or notice, to restrict liability to a specified sum of money` AND `the question arises whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "Where by reference to a contract term or notice a person seeks to restrict liability to a specified sum of money, and the question arises ... whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness, regard shall be had in particular ... to (a) the resources which he could expect to be available to him for the purpose of meeting the liability should it arise; and (b) how far it was open to him to cover himself by insurance"
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the "Where" is not met (ruling of 2026-11-05: a failed "where" on a positive requirement answers JUST FALSE)
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:338 | (4) Where by reference to a contract term or notice a person seeks to
-- src:339 | restrict liability to a specified sum of money, and the question arises
-- src:340 | (under this or any other Act) whether the term or notice satisfies the
-- src:341 | requirement of reasonableness, regard shall be had in particular (but
-- src:342 | without prejudice to subsection (2) in the case of contract terms) to —
-- src:344 | (a) the resources which he could expect to be available to him
-- src:345 | for the purpose of meeting the liability should it arise; and
-- src:347 | (b) how far it was open to him to cover himself by insurance.
@ref s 11(4), the matters (src:338-347)
GIVEN `a person seeks, by reference to a contract term or notice, to restrict liability to a specified sum of money` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the question arises whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `Matter for a capped term`)
`s 11(4) the matters to which regard is had where liability is restricted to a specified sum` MEANS
    IF `s 11(4) regard is to be had in particular to the resources and insurance matters` `a person seeks, by reference to a contract term or notice, to restrict liability to a specified sum of money` `the question arises whether the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST (LIST
            `The resources the party could expect to be available to meet the liability`
            , `How far it was open to the party to cover itself by insurance`)
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the "Where" of subsection (4) is not met; whether regard is had to these matters is then answered by `s 11(4) regard is to be had in particular to the resources and insurance matters`, not by a list
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:349 | (5) It is for those claiming that a contract term or notice satisfies the
-- src:350 | requirement of reasonableness to show that it does.
@ref s 11(5) (src:349-350)
GIVEN `the party claims that the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 11(5) the party must show that the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness` MEANS
    IF `the party claims that the term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness`
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "It is for those claiming that a contract term or notice satisfies the requirement of reasonableness to show that it does"
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): subsection (5) places the burden on those claiming reasonableness; it does not say what the other side must show
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 12 - dealing as consumer`

-- src:354 | 12.—(1) A party to a contract “deals as consumer” in relation to
-- src:355 | another party if —
-- src:357 | (a) he neither makes the contract in the course of a business
-- src:358 | nor holds himself out as doing so;
-- src:360 | (b) the other party does make the contract in the course of a
-- src:361 | business; and
-- src:363 | (c) in the case of a contract governed by the law of sale of
-- src:364 | goods or hire-purchase, or by section 7, the goods passing
-- src:365 | under or in pursuance of the contract are of a type
-- src:366 | ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption.
@ref s 12(1) (src:354-366)
GIVEN dealing IS A `Dealing facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(1) the party deals as consumer in relation to the other party` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF dealing's `the party made the contract in the course of a business` OR dealing's `the party held himself or herself out as making the contract in the course of a business`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words; marginal reading for a lawyer, a failed "if" is the text's no): "(a) he neither makes the contract in the course of a business nor holds himself out as doing so"
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF NOT dealing's `the other party made the contract in the course of a business`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words; marginal reading for a lawyer, a failed "if" is the text's no): "(b) the other party does make the contract in the course of a business"
        THEN JUST FALSE
        IF dealing's `kind of contract` EQUALS `Another kind of contract`
        -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): limbs (a) and (b) are met and limb (c) applies only "in the case of a contract governed by the law of sale of goods or hire-purchase, or by section 7"
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE `limb c`
    WHERE
        `limb c` MEANS
            CONSIDER dealing's `the goods passing are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption`
            -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "(c) in the case of a contract governed by the law of sale of goods or hire-purchase, or by section 7, the goods passing under or in pursuance of the contract are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption"
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words; marginal reading for a lawyer, a failed "if" is the text's no): limb (c) is not met
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether the goods are "of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption" is not given
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:368 | (2) But on a sale by auction or by competitive tender the buyer is
-- src:369 | not in any circumstances to be regarded as dealing as consumer.
@ref s 12(2) (src:368-369)
GIVEN dealing IS A `Dealing facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(2) the buyer is regarded as dealing as consumer` MEANS
    IF dealing's `the party is the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "on a sale by auction or by competitive tender the buyer is not in any circumstances to be regarded as dealing as consumer"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) speaks only of "a sale by auction or by competitive tender"; for another sale it is silent
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:354 | 12.—(1) A party to a contract “deals as consumer” in relation to
-- src:355 | another party if —
-- src:357 | (a) he neither makes the contract in the course of a business
-- src:358 | nor holds himself out as doing so;
-- src:360 | (b) the other party does make the contract in the course of a
-- src:361 | business; and
-- src:363 | (c) in the case of a contract governed by the law of sale of
-- src:364 | goods or hire-purchase, or by section 7, the goods passing
-- src:365 | under or in pursuance of the contract are of a type
-- src:366 | ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption.
-- src:368 | (2) But on a sale by auction or by competitive tender the buyer is
-- src:369 | not in any circumstances to be regarded as dealing as consumer.
@ref s 12(1) and (2) (src:354-369)
GIVEN dealing IS A `Dealing facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(1) and (2) the party deals as consumer` MEANS
    IF `s 12(2) the buyer is regarded as dealing as consumer` dealing EQUALS JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "But on a sale by auction or by competitive tender the buyer is not in any circumstances to be regarded as dealing as consumer" -- overrides subsection (1)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE `s 12(1) the party deals as consumer in relation to the other party` dealing

-- src:371 | (3) Subject to this, it is for those claiming that a party does not deal
-- src:372 | as consumer to show that he does not.
@ref s 12(3) (src:371-372)
GIVEN `the party claims that a party does not deal as consumer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the party is the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12(3) the party must show that a party does not deal as consumer` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the party is the buyer on a sale by auction or by competitive tender`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Subject to this" -- subsection (2) governs a sale by auction or competitive tender, where the buyer is never a consumer, and subsection (3) says nothing about a burden there
        THEN NOTHING
        IF `the party claims that a party does not deal as consumer`
        -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "it is for those claiming that a party does not deal as consumer to show that he does not"
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): subsection (3) places the burden on those claiming; it does not say what the party claiming to be a consumer must show
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `Section 13 - varieties of exemption clause`

-- src:377 | 13.—(1) To the extent that this Part prevents the exclusion or
-- src:378 | restriction of any liability it also prevents —
-- src:380 | (a) making the liability or its enforcement subject to restrictive
-- src:381 | or onerous conditions;
-- src:383 | (b) excluding or restricting any right or remedy in respect of
-- src:384 | the liability, or subjecting a person to any prejudice in
-- src:385 | consequence of his pursuing any such right or remedy;
-- src:387 | (c) excluding or restricting rules of evidence or procedure,
-- src:389 | and (to that extent) sections 2 and 5 to 7 also prevent excluding or
-- src:390 | restricting liability by reference to terms and notices which exclude or
-- src:391 | restrict the relevant obligation or duty.
@ref s 13(1) (src:377-391)
GIVEN variety IS A `Variety of exemption clause`
      `this Part prevents the exclusion or restriction of the liability` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the section that prevents it` IS A `Section of Part 1`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 13(1) the person can use the exemption clause to avoid the liability` MEANS
    CONSIDER `this Part prevents the exclusion or restriction of the liability`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        CONSIDER variety
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "To the extent that this Part prevents the exclusion or restriction of any liability it also prevents (a) making the liability or its enforcement subject to restrictive or onerous conditions"
        WHEN `Makes the liability or its enforcement subject to restrictive or onerous conditions` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) excluding or restricting any right or remedy in respect of the liability, or subjecting a person to any prejudice in consequence of his pursuing any such right or remedy"
        WHEN `Excludes or restricts a right or remedy in respect of the liability, or subjects a person to prejudice for pursuing it` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(c) excluding or restricting rules of evidence or procedure"
        WHEN `Excludes or restricts rules of evidence or procedure` THEN JUST FALSE
        WHEN `Excludes or restricts the relevant obligation or duty itself` THEN
            CONSIDER `the section that prevents it`
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and (to that extent) sections 2 and 5 to 7 also prevent excluding or restricting liability by reference to terms and notices which exclude or restrict the relevant obligation or duty"
            WHEN `Section 2(1)` THEN JUST FALSE
            WHEN `Section 2(2)` THEN JUST FALSE
            WHEN `Section 5` THEN JUST FALSE
            WHEN `Section 6` THEN JUST FALSE
            WHEN `Section 7` THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "sections 2 and 5 to 7" -- sections 3 and 4 are not among them
            WHEN `Section 3` THEN NOTHING
            WHEN `Section 4` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) speaks of an arbitration agreement only in subsection (2); this is not one of the varieties (a) to (c) or the exclusion of the obligation itself
        WHEN `An agreement in writing to submit present or future differences to arbitration` THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): another variety is not one the subsection names
        WHEN `Another variety` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "To the extent that this Part prevents the exclusion or restriction of any liability" -- this Part does not prevent it, so subsection (1) says nothing
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether this Part prevents the exclusion or restriction of the liability is not given
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:393 | (2) But an agreement in writing to submit present or future
-- src:394 | differences to arbitration is not to be treated under this Part as
-- src:395 | excluding or restricting any liability.
@ref s 13(2) (src:393-395)
GIVEN variety IS A `Variety of exemption clause`
      `the agreement is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 13(2) the agreement is treated under this Part as excluding or restricting liability` MEANS
    IF variety EQUALS `An agreement in writing to submit present or future differences to arbitration` AND `the agreement is in writing`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an agreement in writing to submit present or future differences to arbitration is not to be treated under this Part as excluding or restricting any liability"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (2) speaks only of "an agreement in writing to submit present or future differences to arbitration"
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 14 - interpretation of this Part`

-- src:399 | 14. In this Part —
-- src:400 | “business” includes a profession and the activities of any
-- src:401 | Government department or local or public authority;
-- src:403 | “goods” has the same meaning as in the Sale of Goods Act 1979;
-- src:405 | “hire-purchase agreement” has the same meaning as in the
-- src:406 | Hire-Purchase Act 1969;
-- src:408 | “negligence” has the meaning given to it by section 1(1);
-- src:409 | “notice” includes an announcement, whether or not in writing,
-- src:411 | and any other communication or pretended communication;
-- src:413 | “personal injury” includes any disease and any impairment of
-- src:414 | physical or mental condition.
@ref s 14 (src:399-414)
GIVEN word IS A `Defined word`
GIVETH A `Definition entry`
`s 14 and ss 1, 5, 12 and 29(3) what the Act says the word means` MEANS
    CONSIDER word
    WHEN business THEN `Definition entry` OF business, "s 14", Includes, (LIST "a profession", "the activities of any Government department or local or public authority"), ""
    WHEN goods THEN `Definition entry` OF goods, "s 14", `Has the same meaning as in another Act`, EMPTY, "Sale of Goods Act 1979"
    WHEN `hire-purchase agreement` THEN `Definition entry` OF `hire-purchase agreement`, "s 14", `Has the same meaning as in another Act`, EMPTY, "Hire-Purchase Act 1969"
    WHEN negligence THEN `Definition entry` OF negligence, "s 14", `Has the meaning given by a section`, EMPTY, "section 1(1)"
    WHEN notice THEN `Definition entry` OF notice, "s 14", Includes, (LIST "an announcement, whether or not in writing", "any other communication or pretended communication"), ""
    WHEN `personal injury` THEN `Definition entry` OF `personal injury`, "s 14", Includes, (LIST "any disease", "any impairment of physical or mental condition"), ""
    WHEN `competent authority` THEN `Definition entry` OF `competent authority`, "s 29(3)", Means, (LIST "any court", "arbitrator or arbiter", "Government department", "public authority"), ""
    WHEN enactment THEN `Definition entry` OF enactment, "s 29(3)", Means, (LIST "any legislation (including subsidiary legislation) of Singapore", "any instrument having effect by virtue of such legislation"), ""
    WHEN statutory THEN `Definition entry` OF statutory, "s 29(3)", Means, EMPTY, "conferred by an enactment"
    WHEN `deals as consumer` THEN `Definition entry` OF `deals as consumer`, "s 12(1)", `Is defined by a list of conditions`, (LIST "he neither makes the contract in the course of a business nor holds himself out as doing so", "the other party does make the contract in the course of a business", "in the case of a contract governed by the law of sale of goods or hire-purchase, or by section 7, the goods passing under or in pursuance of the contract are of a type ordinarily supplied for private use or consumption"), ""
    WHEN `in consumer use` THEN `Definition entry` OF `in consumer use`, "s 5(2)(a)", `Is defined by a list of conditions`, (LIST "a person is using them, or has them in his possession for use, otherwise than exclusively for the purposes of a business"), ""
    WHEN guarantee THEN `Definition entry` OF guarantee, "s 5(2)(b)", `Is defined by a list of conditions`, (LIST "anything in writing", "contains or purports to contain some promise or assurance (however worded or presented) that defects will be made good by complete or partial replacement, or by repair, monetary compensation or otherwise"), ""
    WHEN `business liability` THEN `Definition entry` OF `business liability`, "s 1(3)", Means, (LIST "liability for breach of obligations or duties arising from things done or to be done by a person in the course of a business (whether his own business or another's)"), ""
    WHEN company THEN `Definition entry` OF company, "sch 1 para 1(d)(i)", Means, (LIST "any body corporate", "any unincorporated association", "a partnership"), ""

-- src:400 | “business” includes a profession and the activities of any
-- src:401 | Government department or local or public authority;
@ref s 14, "business" (src:400-401)
GIVEN activity IS A `Kind of activity`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 14 the activity is within "business"` MEANS
    CONSIDER activity
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "business includes a profession and the activities of any Government department or local or public authority"
    WHEN `A profession` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `The activities of a Government department` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `The activities of a local authority` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `The activities of a public authority` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "business includes" -- the list is not closed
    WHEN `Another activity` THEN NOTHING

-- src:413 | “personal injury” includes any disease and any impairment of
-- src:414 | physical or mental condition.
@ref s 14, "personal injury" (src:413-414)
GIVEN harm IS A `Kind of harm`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 14 the harm is within "personal injury"` MEANS
    CONSIDER harm
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "personal injury includes any disease and any impairment of physical or mental condition"
    WHEN `A disease` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `An impairment of physical or mental condition` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "personal injury includes" -- the Act names a disease and an impairment of condition and does not say what else is personal injury
    WHEN Death THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): as above; a bodily injury is not named
    WHEN `A bodily injury` THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): as above; damage to property is not named
    WHEN `Damage to property or another loss` THEN NOTHING

-- src:409 | “notice” includes an announcement, whether or not in writing,
-- src:411 | and any other communication or pretended communication;
@ref s 14, "notice" (src:409-411)
GIVEN communication IS A `Kind of communication`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 14 the communication is within "notice"` MEANS
    CONSIDER communication
    -- TRUE-kind (the text says yes in its own words): "notice includes an announcement, whether or not in writing, and any other communication or pretended communication"
    WHEN `An announcement, in writing` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `An announcement, not in writing` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `Another communication` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `A pretended communication` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "notice includes" -- the list is not closed, and something that is not a communication is not named
    WHEN `Something else` THEN NOTHING

